artifact.self_contained_problem:
Let $C \subset \mathbb{R}^3$ be a non-circular, simple closed convex planar curve lying in a plane $P$. A compact embedded constant-mean-curvature (CMC) disk $\Sigma$ spanning $C$ is a compact embedded surface in $\mathbb{R}^3$ that is topologically a disk, has constant mean curvature, and whose boundary equals $C$. For such a $\Sigma$, let $V = V(\Sigma) > 0$ denote the (signed) volume enclosed between $\Sigma$ and the planar disk bounded by $C$ in $P$.

Prove or disprove: there exists a threshold value $V_C > 0$, depending only on $C$, such that for every compact embedded CMC disk $\Sigma$ spanning $C$ with enclosed volume $V$, exactly one of the following holds:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] if $V \leq V_C$, then $\Sigma$ lies entirely in one of the two closed half-spaces determined by $P$;
\item[(ii)] if $V = V_C$, then $\Sigma$ is tangent to $P$ precisely at the points of $C$ where the planar curvature of $C$ attains its minimum;
\item[(iii)] if $V > V_C$, then $\Sigma$ contains points strictly on both sides of $P$.
\end{enumerate}

